    {
      "tag": "external_alliance",
      "layer": "external_political",
      "description": "Granada's diplomatic skill in playing Castile, Aragon, and the Marinids of Morocco against each other sustained the emirate for 260 years; tribute payments and calculated submission to Castile avoided direct conquest while Christian kingdoms remained internally divided",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q238445",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q238445"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Emirate of Granada",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_Granada"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "layer": "geography_territory",
      "tag": "geographic_defensibility",
      "direction": "rise",
      "description": "Granada's mountain terrain, the Sierra Nevada rising to 3,478m, made it extraordinarily difficult to conquer. The Nasrid dynasty survived 250 years by exploiting these natural fortifications and maintaining castles at strategic mountain passes.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q238445",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q238445"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Emirate of Granada",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_Granada"
        }
      ],
      "tag_prose": "sierra_nevada_defensibility"
    },
    {
      "layer": "economic_commercial",
      "tag": "trade_route_control",
      "direction": "rise",
      "description": "The Alpujarra mountain valleys produced the finest raw silk in Western Europe. Granada's silk revenues funded military defenses and allowed payment of tribute to Castile when necessary to defer Christian attack.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q238445",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q238445"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Emirate of Granada",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_Granada"
        }
      ],
      "tag_prose": "silk_production_revenues"
    },
    {
      "layer": "ideology_religion",
      "tag": "alhambra_palatial_complex",
      "direction": "rise",
      "description": "The Alhambra palace complex (begun 1238, elaborated 14th c.) combined military, administrative, and aesthetic functions. Its sophisticated hydraulic engineering and mathematical geometric decoration demonstrated high cultural achievement that deterred simple destruction.",
      "sources": [
        {
          "type": "wikidata",
          "id": "Q238445",
          "url": "https://www.wikidata.org/wiki/Q238445"
        },
        {
          "type": "wikipedia",
          "label": "Emirate of Granada",
          "url": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Emirate_of_Granada"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "layer": "external_political",
      "tag": "castilian_client_state_diplomacy",
      "direction": "rise",
      "description": "The Nasrid emirs skillfully played Castile and Aragon against each other while occasionally aiding Castile against other enemies (including at the Salado battle 1340). This diplomacy extended the emirate's life by several generations.",
      "sources": [
        {
